 ],
 "note": "Rests differ by a single hook -- one for an eighth, two for a sixteenth -- and a contact sheet shows them at a fixed size with no staff around them, so they were read at a glance and three came out wrong. They are counted now, against the families where the two are already told apart. Getting one wrong is worth a whole bar: a sixteenth rest read as an eighth leaves the bar an eighth too long and nothing else on the page contradicts it.\nA filled notehead with a stroke through it and a wavy tremolo are both wide, dark and roughly twice as long as they are tall, and on a contact sheet at one size they are easy to confuse. One of them was labelled a tremolo, and the label decides the role: a tremolo is inert, so the note was not counted as an attack at all and vanished from its bar. Cropped from a real page the two are not alike -- the slashed head sits on the staff with a stem, the tremolo floats above the note it belongs to."
}